+// A frame normally reaches presentation a little after its intended time: the GPU thread takes the
+// swap up when it gets to it, and how long that takes changes from frame to frame (by 1-4 ms in
+// busy scenes). Presenting each frame on arrival hands that jitter to the display, where at a
+// refresh rate that is a multiple of the game's (120 Hz) a frame 1-2 ms off lands on the wrong
+// refresh and stays up for one or three refreshes instead of two. So every frame is held to one
+// common delay after its intended time instead: the delay rises at once to a frame that arrives
+// later than it, and decays slowly while frames arrive earlier.
+DT Presenter::UpdateLatePresentationDelay(DT lateness)
+{
+  if (lateness > MAX_LATE_PRESENTATION_DELAY)
+    return lateness;
+
+  lateness = std::max(lateness, DT{});
+  if (lateness > m_late_presentation_delay)
+    m_late_presentation_delay = lateness;
+  else
+    m_late_presentation_delay -= (m_late_presentation_delay - lateness) / LATE_PRESENTATION_DECAY;
+
+  return m_late_presentation_delay;
+}
